          <p>Gross figures use the standard full-time year of 2,080 hours (40 hours \u00d7 52 weeks). Monthly averages divide by 12; biweekly figures use 26 paychecks. After-tax figures use 2026 federal rules for a single filer taking the standard deduction: $16,100 deduction, brackets 10% to $12,400, 12% to $50,400, 22% to $105,700 of taxable income (IRS Revenue Procedure 2025-32). FICA is 7.65% (Social Security 6.2% to the $184,500 wage base, Medicare 1.45%). The 1099 figures use self-employment tax at 15.3% on 92.35% of earnings, with half of it deducted before the same brackets. Median wage: Bureau of Labor Statistics, Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics, May 2025 ($24.51 an hour); 75th-percentile wage $38.71 an hour (BLS, May 2025); median weekly earnings $1,251 in the second quarter of 2026 (BLS). Competitor note: DollarCalcs matches the $81,120 gross and $65,898 take-home but calls the 18.8% combined federal-plus-FICA rate the effective federal rate (the federal rate alone is 11.1%) and prices no raise, overtime, or unpaid-week cases. FinalRoundAI contradicts itself on the monthly figure ($5,454.49 against the correct $5,491.49) and the federal tax ($9,460.40 against $9,016.40). Paycheckscalculator.org shows a $2,307.33 biweekly net in its widget against $65,898 in its own text. This article is general information, not tax or legal advice.</p>
